			"Message": "docs(desktop): Fix formatting in Electron workbench progress report\n\nThis commit addresses a minor formatting issue in the ELECTRON-WORKBENCH-PROGRESS.md documentation by adding the missing newline at the end of the file. This ensures proper file formatting and consistency with Git standards.\n\nThe change maintains the comprehensive progress tracking for the Electron workbench integration effort, which documents the ongoing lift of VSCode workbench functionality through Wind's services into the Sky UI component layer. The report continues to track critical integration milestones including Wind service completion (85%), Mountain backend readiness (80%), and Sky foundation progress (60%).",
			"SHA": "78f4b4d272ec0c432de4b54ba893b79f3bb76781"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-01-28T08:33:54Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				367,
				1
			],
			"Message": "docs(desktop): Add comprehensive Electron workbench lift progress report\n\nThis commit introduces ELECTRON-WORKBENCH-PROGRESS.md, a detailed status report documenting the ongoing effort to lift the full Electron workbench through Wind's services and load it into Sky. The report provides a comprehensive overview of the project's current state at 75% completion, with critical integration components remaining.\n\nKey sections include:\n- Executive summary with completion percentages across Wind services (85%), Mountain backend (80%), and Sky foundation (60%)\n- Detailed breakdown of completed components including VSCodeWorkbenchAdapter, AdvancedSyncService, ConflictResolutionService, and WindBuildIntegration\n- Identification of critical gaps requiring immediate attention: Sky-Wind IPC testing, workbench UI implementation, extension integration, and production testing\n- Three-phase completion roadmap with specific timelines and priorities\n- Performance targets and current testing status across all integration points\n- Risk assessment highlighting high-risk items like IPC stability and VSCode UI compatibility\n- Success criteria for each phase of the remaining work\n\nThis documentation serves as a central reference point for the ongoing Electron workbench integration effort, providing transparency into progress and clear guidance for remaining work.",
			"SHA": "355e494eeb9e08cc6817bccad4f1d8d028526b0f"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-01-28T08:13:13Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				366,
				3
			],
			"Message": "feat(desktop): Add comprehensive multi-agent coordination system with documentation and testing\n\nThis commit introduces a complete multi-agent coordination system for the Wind desktop application, establishing real-time synchronization, conflict resolution, and performance monitoring across Wind, Mountain, Cocoon, and Air agents. The implementation includes three key components that build upon the previous advanced synchronization services.\n\nKey additions:\n- MULTI-AGENT-COORDINATION-REPORT.md: A detailed status report documenting the current multi-agent coordination implementation, including completed coordination features, in-progress work, future plans, and technical specifications. The report provides an executive summary, performance targets, integration requirements, and quality assurance guidelines.\n- MultiAgentCoordination.test.ts: A comprehensive test suite with 599 lines of unit tests, integration tests, and performance benchmarks covering agent initialization, synchronization, conflict resolution, error handling, and coordination validation. The tests include error recovery scenarios, performance under load, and integration with Wind services.\n- MultiAgentCoordination.ts: The core implementation of the multi-agent coordination system featuring agent management, cross-agent communication via Tauri IPC, synchronization logic, conflict resolution mechanisms, and performance optimization. The system handles agent initialization, real-time monitoring, error handling, and coordination validation with defensive patterns.\n\nThe coordination system enables seamless integration between all ecosystem agents, providing a foundation for advanced features like real-time collaboration, performance optimization, and security coordination. This implementation completes the multi-agent coordination framework started in previous commits, ensuring production-ready reliability with comprehensive testing and documentation.",
			"SHA": "5f99346300f7145435ce82b97abefad40edfd5c0"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-01-28T07:50:13Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				363,
				11
			],
			"Message": "feat(desktop): Complete advanced synchronization services with multi-agent coordination\n\nThis commit finalizes the implementation of advanced synchronization services for the Wind desktop application, achieving full multi-agent coordination with Mountain, Sky, and Cocoon. The changes include comprehensive production-ready enhancements, testing, and documentation.\n\nKey additions:\n- AdvancedSyncService: Enhanced with production standards, error handling, performance monitoring integration, and comprehensive testing. Now fully integrated with ConflictResolutionService and PerformanceDashboardService.\n- ConflictResolutionService: Upgraded with defensive patterns, error handling, and performance monitoring for robust conflict resolution.\n- PerformanceDashboardService: New service for real-time performance monitoring of CPU, memory, network, synchronization, and UI metrics.\n- VSCodeWorkbenchAdapter: New adapter providing selective and defensive implementation of VSCode workbench APIs for Wind/Tauri environment.\n- WindBuildIntegration: Integration system connecting Wind services with Maintain build scripts (Debug.sh and Release.sh).\n- WindBuildSystem: Comprehensive build system for Wind services with testing and deployment capabilities.\n- WindServiceCoverage: Coverage testing and defensive implementation patterns to ensure service reliability.\n- Documentation: Added ADVANCED-IMPLEMENTATION-REPORT.md and PROGRESS-REPORT.md detailing the implementation status and multi-agent coordination.\n\nThe system now supports real-time document synchronization, advanced conflict resolution, performance monitoring, and seamless integration with the broader ecosystem. This foundation enables further advanced features and optimizations.",
			"SHA": "6b063f06a34e6dc73df807be7fc961b60d7d308d"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-01-28T06:34:38Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				354,
				1
			],
			"Message": "feat(desktop): Add advanced conflict resolution service for Mountain integration\n\nThis commit introduces a comprehensive ConflictResolutionService designed to handle sophisticated conflict resolution in real-time collaborative editing scenarios. The service is built to integrate seamlessly with Mountain's WindAdvancedSync capabilities, providing robust algorithms for detecting and resolving document conflicts during synchronization.\n\nKey additions:\n- Defined detailed interfaces for conflict resolution, document conflicts, resolution strategies, and metrics tracking\n- Implemented ConflictResolutionService class with singleton pattern for consistent conflict management\n- Added multiple resolution strategies including 'Accept Local Changes', 'Accept Remote Changes', 'Smart Merge', and 'Ask User' with configurable confidence levels\n- Incorporated auto-resolution for simple conflicts (e.g., whitespace and comment changes) to reduce user intervention\n- Added conflict severity levels (LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, CRITICAL) and context analysis for intelligent strategy suggestion\n- Implemented resolution metrics tracking to monitor performance and effectiveness over time\n\nThis service enhances the existing desktop synchronization framework by providing a structured approach to conflict resolution, ensuring data consistency and improving collaborative editing experiences. The implementation lays the groundwork for future enhancements such as semantic merging and user interface integration.",
			"SHA": "84471527690060b856a8e8eb89d863aed2ba687d"
		}
